course: dictionary
lesson: 13150
title: Forewarn: warned before the danger
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Forewarn is made of two parts: fore, which means before, and warn, which means tell someone about a danger. So to forewarn someone is to warn them before something bad happens, so they have time to get ready. «The weather office forewarned us that a storm was coming.» The storm had not arrived yet. The warning came first.
explain: Forewarn is a verb, so its form can change: forewarns, forewarned, forewarning. «Mum forewarns me when it is going to be cold.» «The teacher had forewarned the class about the test.» In every case, the warning comes before the bad thing.
explain: An old saying uses this word: «Forewarned is forearmed.» It means that if you know about a danger early, you can prepare for it. Knowing in advance protects you, a bit like wearing armour.
explain: Forewarn is a little formal. You will meet it more often in stories and news than in chatty talk. Everyday speakers often say warn instead, but the idea is the same: a warning that gives you time.
fact: Forewarn means to warn someone before something bad or difficult happens.
fact: A forewarning gives people time to get ready.
fact: «Forewarned is forearmed» means that knowing about a danger early helps you prepare.
fact: «We were forewarned about the storm.»
fact: Forewarn is a verb, so it can be forewarns, forewarned, or forewarning.
exercise: «The map forewarned us about the steep hill ahead.» What does forewarned mean here? | Forgot the way completely | Ran down the hill very fast | *Warned before, so we could get ready | Took a photo of the hill
exercise: Which saying means that knowing about a danger early helps you prepare? | *Forewarned is forearmed | Many hands make light work | Too many cooks spoil the broth | Every cloud has a silver lining
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to forewarn? | Celebrate | *Warn in advance | Forget | Surprise
test: «The lifeguard forewarned the swimmers about the strong waves.» What did the lifeguard do? | Waited until someone was hurt | *Warned them before the danger, so they could prepare | Made the waves stronger | Told them the waves were a joke
test: «Yesterday, the coach ___ us about the rain.» Which form of forewarn fits the gap? | forewarn | forewarning | *forewarned | forewarns
exam: Which is the best meaning of forewarn? | To hide a danger from people | To fight off a danger | To happen after something bad | *To warn someone before something bad happens
